WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:02.880
Sometimes, it's hard to tell&nbsp;
the difference between Easter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:07.040
eggs and clues that video games share a&nbsp;
universe. Just so happens that, sometimes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:12.000
they're both. Let's take a closer look at some&nbsp;
video game universes with startling connections.

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:16.880
The Uncharted and The Last of Us series are two&nbsp;
of PlayStation's biggest and best first-party&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:22.720
exclusives. But did you know that one is actually&nbsp;
a sequel to the other? Well… kind of, at least.

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:25.840
In Uncharted 3, there's a&nbsp;
newspaper with a headline reading:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:30.400
"Scientists are still struggling to understand&nbsp;
deadly fungus" — an intentional reference to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:34.640
The Last of Us. At the time of Uncharted&nbsp;
3's release, however, The Last of Us had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:39.920
yet to be formally announced, and that newspaper&nbsp;
wasn't actually supposed to remain in the game.

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:43.200
Naughty Dog's Neil Druckmann&nbsp;
candidly told Kotaku :

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:46.080
"We just completely forgot&nbsp;
about it. We screwed up."

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:48.640
Fortunately for them, they&nbsp;
ended up getting away with it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:50.960
without spoiling their then-upcoming announcement.

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:53.040
"What took you so long?"

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:54.320
"What took you so long?"

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:55.840
"What took you so long?"

00:00:55.840 --> 00:01:00.400
Japanese video game designer Hideo Kojima&nbsp;
likes to fill his games with Easter eggs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:04.240
And that's why his graphic adventure&nbsp;
game Policenauts offers plenty of clues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:07.280
that it shares a universe with&nbsp;
Snatcher and Metal Gear Solid.

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:12.560
Policenauts has tons of Snatcher references. In&nbsp;
addition to a few direct cameos from the cyberpunk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:18.000
adventure game, there's a Snatcher calendar in Jun&nbsp;
Ishida's office, and Victor Jurgens uses the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:22.960
brand of sunscreen found in both games. There's&nbsp;
also a Snatcher target in the police station's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:28.080
shooting range that plays a remixed version of the&nbsp;
game's "Pressure of Tension" song when it's hit.

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:33.040
In Metal Gear Solid, you can find two promotional&nbsp;
posters for Policenauts in the Nuclear Warhead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:37.360
Storage Building, with a third popping up&nbsp;
in Metal Gear Solid 2: Sons of Liberty.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:43.120
Drebin 893 drinks a soda called is named&nbsp;
after the addictive drug in Policenauts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:46.800
and there's even a bar named after&nbsp;
Solid Snake in Policenauts' Old L.A.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:50.400
And that's just scratching the surface&nbsp;
of the cosmic clues — happy hunting!

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:55.680
The Red Faction series from Volition and&nbsp;
THQ tells the story of the titular faction's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.680 --> 00:02:00.000
resistance against the oppressive reign&nbsp;
of the nefarious Ultor Corporation, set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:05.360
amidst a sci-fi future of dystopian proportions.&nbsp;
Meanwhile, the Saints Row series from Volition and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:11.040
THQ — along with latter day publisher Deep Silver&nbsp;
— chronicles the story of the Third Street Saints,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:16.560
a street gang turned global crime organization who&nbsp;
face rival criminals and corrupt law enforcement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:20.800
during their ongoing slapstick quest for&nbsp;
domination. While the first game in the series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:26.240
was a relatively straightforward GTA clone,&nbsp;
later entities… well, they got a little wild.

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:31.280
"You can't kill me! My&nbsp;
people will avenge my death!"

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:34.080
"Not with a bang… but with a whimper."

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:37.200
As luck — and the cheeky developers&nbsp;
of Volition — would have it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:41.920
the events of the Red Faction series actually take&nbsp;
place in the future of the Saints Row universe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:44.800
and the nefarious Ultor Corporation is the link.

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:48.160
"The Ultor corporation has been using a secret R&amp;D&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:52.640
lab to conduct unethical experiments&nbsp;
on the citizens of Stilwater."

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:57.360
In case you missed it, the episodic expansion&nbsp;
packs Ultor Exposed and Corporate Warfare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:02.800
for Saints Row 2 are probably the most overt&nbsp;
references to Red Faction's big bad adversaries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:07.680
in the Saints series. The first of these two&nbsp;
DLC packs lays the groundwork for exposing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:12.560
megacorp's seedy operations, while the second&nbsp;
installment offers players a chance to, quote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:16.080
"put a permanent end to Ultor's&nbsp;
corruption" head on. The result:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:19.520
one of the craziest canonical&nbsp;
crossovers in video game history.

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:24.240
Remedy Entertainment's connected universe&nbsp;
is more intricate than many fans realize.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:28.960
Remedy's 2010 game Alan Wake is a bizarre trip&nbsp;
through alternate dimensions. As the title&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:33.760
character searches for his missing wife, he begins&nbsp;
to see elements of a story he wrote coming to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:38.880
life. The endings of Alan Wake and its follow-ups&nbsp;
left its hero's fate unclear, which has tormented&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:43.840
fans for years. But did you know references to&nbsp;
Mr. Wake have shown up in Remedy's newer titles?

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:48.640
Quantum Break, released in 2016, features&nbsp;
several callbacks to Alan Wake. For instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:51.760
a chalkboard in the game seems to&nbsp;
describe events from Alan Wake.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:56.320
Not only that, another Easter egg revealed&nbsp;
live action footage of Alan Wake himself.

00:03:56.320 --> 00:04:00.640
Then came 2019's Control. Through&nbsp;
documents discovered during Control,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:05.680
players learn that the game's Federal Bureau of&nbsp;
Control investigated the events of Alan Wake, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:10.880
unfortunately didn't find much. Footage of Alan&nbsp;
can also be found in a hidden area of the game.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:16.080
Finally, Control's AWE expansion brought&nbsp;
Bureau Director Jesse Faden into conflict&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:20.320
with Emil Hartman, a mad psychologist&nbsp;
with ties to Alan and Alice Wake.

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:25.760
The Malaysia-inspired island of Panau is the&nbsp;
setting of Just Cause 2, the 2010 open world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:30.960
action-adventure game from Avalanche Studios and&nbsp;
Eidos Interactive . It tells the continuing story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:36.080
of chaotic hero Rico Rodriguez and his battle&nbsp;
against some of the world's craziest militant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:41.280
dictators. Interestingly enough, Panau also makes&nbsp;
a subtle appearance in Sleeping Dogs, the Hong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:46.800
Kong-based action adventure from United Front&nbsp;
Games and Eidos parent publisher Square Enix .

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:51.440
One Easter egg from Sleeping Dogs definitively&nbsp;
places these two games in the same universe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:54.880
If your timing and radio station are&nbsp;
correct, you'll hear a fun little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.880 --> 00:04:58.400
advertisement that shouts out the&nbsp;
familiar setting of Just Cause 2:

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:00.320
"For a relaxing getaway,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:07.120
visit the island of Panau, an exotic location&nbsp;
where beauty and luxury are an everyday pleasure."

00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:11.760
French video game publisher Ubisoft made&nbsp;
its reputation by developing some of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:16.480
most immersive open world games on the market —&nbsp;
games that emphasize exploration and branching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.480 --> 00:05:20.640
narratives over linear storytelling and&nbsp;
guided adventures. It's no surprise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:24.240
then, that Ubisoft would decide to set&nbsp;
a few of their fan-favorite franchises&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:27.440
in a shared universe; but the&nbsp;
clues aren't always up front.

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:32.880
Fortunately for us, the modern-day sequences of&nbsp;
Assassin's Creed 4: Black Flag offer some clarity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:37.520
Set in the offices of a video game development&nbsp;
studio known as Abstergo Entertainment — a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:42.560
not-so-subtle front for Abstergo Industries,&nbsp;
aka the Templar Order, the Assassins' rival&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:49.120
faction — these sequences provide several distinct&nbsp;
references to Ubisoft's Watch Dogs and Far Cry 3.

00:05:49.120 --> 00:05:51.520
For instance, the "Security Systems Presentation"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:56.160
offers a blatant reference to the primary&nbsp;
city-wide hacking mechanic of Watch Dogs:

00:05:56.160 --> 00:06:00.000
"Nowhere is our expertise more&nbsp;
apparent than in our flagship product,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:04.960
'ctOS ' – a ground-breaking proprietary&nbsp;
security-software operating system."

00:06:04.960 --> 00:06:10.160
Meanwhile, the Abstergo Entertainment database&nbsp;
entry for "Ocelot" obliquely suggests that Far&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.160 --> 00:06:15.440
Cry 3, the video game itself, could actually&nbsp;
be yet another virtual construct of the Animus.

00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:19.840
"Did I ever tell you the definition of insanity?"

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:25.440
The science-fiction stylings of NieR: Automata&nbsp;
from PlatinumGames and publisher Square Enix&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:29.600
seem like a far cry from the medieval&nbsp;
fantasy of Drakengard from Cavia Inc.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:34.640
and Macrospace — but these two action-RPGs&nbsp;
are separated by a lengthy span of sequels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.640 --> 00:06:39.120
and studio changeovers, so they actually&nbsp;
exist squarely in the same universe.

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:44.080
The description of NieR: Automata on the game's&nbsp;
official website tells us its story takes place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.080 --> 00:06:48.640
in "the distant future." That future is, in&nbsp;
fact, a far-flung time period following the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.640 --> 00:06:54.400
events of Drakengard, which chronicles the dark&nbsp;
fantasy story of the deposed prince Caim and his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:58.960
efforts to stop the malevolent Watchers from&nbsp;
destroying the world. Game scholars understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.960 --> 00:07:03.840
the "E" ending of Drakengard — in which Caim and&nbsp;
his dragon pal Angelus travel across a dimensional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:08.800
boundary to fight the Queen Grotesquerie in modern&nbsp;
day Tokyo — to be the canonical ending that leads&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:14.560
into the 2010 hack-and-slash RPG Nier, which is&nbsp;
itself a direct forerunner of NieR: Automata.

00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:16.800
Don't worry if you're confused.&nbsp;
You're not the only one.

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:21.440
In NieR: Automata, quote, "Mankind is driven&nbsp;
from Earth and takes refuge on the Moon."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.440 --> 00:07:25.520
Now, with the full history laid out before&nbsp;
us, we finally understand that particular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.520 --> 00:07:29.040
Earth to be the alternate-history&nbsp;
Earth of Drakengard, known as Midgard.

00:07:29.600 --> 00:07:32.720
With ample evidence of a UFO&nbsp;
conspiracy that interlinks the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.720 --> 00:07:36.560
background narratives of Grand Theft&nbsp;
Auto 5 and Red Dead Redemption 2,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.560 --> 00:07:40.560
it's safe to say that Rockstar Games&nbsp;
is no stranger to shared game worlds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:45.760
But did you know that Manhunt from Rockstar North&nbsp;
was also set in the GTA universe? A variety of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.760 --> 00:07:50.960
evidence suggests that it is: notably, the very&nbsp;
location of the game's controversial narrative.

00:07:50.960 --> 00:07:54.400
Manhunt takes place within the&nbsp;
fictional setting of Carcer City,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.400 --> 00:08:00.080
a ruthless lampoon of America's Rust Belt, where&nbsp;
street gangs rule the night. GTA fans who paid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.080 --> 00:08:04.880
enough attention will probably recognize Carcer&nbsp;
City from one of its many subtle appearances in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.880 --> 00:08:10.080
the series; it's been mentioned at least once&nbsp;
in every Grand Theft Auto release since GTA 3,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.080 --> 00:08:13.360
including the Stories spinoffs&nbsp;
developed for handheld consoles.

00:08:13.360 --> 00:08:14.880
"In nearby Carcer City,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.640 --> 00:08:21.840
a good day for order as police chief Gary&nbsp;
Schafer was cleared of corruption charges."

00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:25.120
And if there was any doubt, James&nbsp;
Earl Cash — Manhunt's sociopathic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.120 --> 00:08:29.280
protagonist — appears on wanted posters&nbsp;
within the Grand Theft Auto universe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.280 --> 00:08:33.760
most notably in locations like the Police&nbsp;
Department in San Andreas . Crime might not pay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.760 --> 00:08:36.560
but in Rockstar's shared worlds,&nbsp;
it'll definitely make you famous.

00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:41.280
With its focus on a single-player campaign,&nbsp;
Battlefield: Bad Company from DICE was a bit of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.280 --> 00:08:47.040
a departure from the tried and true formula of the&nbsp;
fan-favorite FPS franchise in 2008. But the game's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:52.240
humorous narrative, along with its introduction of&nbsp;
highly destructible environments, made it a smash&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.240 --> 00:08:57.200
hit. GameSpot called it, quote, "quite simply one&nbsp;
of the most fun shooters released [that] year."

00:08:57.200 --> 00:09:02.000
DICE was also responsible for Mirror's Edge:&nbsp;
Catalyst, the 2016 sequel of their unique&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:08.160
sci-fi action platformer, also from 2008. Catalyst&nbsp;
expanded the story and gameplay of the original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.160 --> 00:09:12.000
and took a few narrative liberties in its&nbsp;
effort to round out the lore behind Faith&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:16.640
Connors and her death-defying run through The&nbsp;
City. DICE looked to their previous titles to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.640 --> 00:09:21.360
establish a "real world" element for the dystopian&nbsp;
future of Mirror's Edge by placing the boys from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:25.680
Bad Company directly into the expanded&nbsp;
narrative of Catalyst via audio files.

00:09:25.680 --> 00:09:28.720
"Did you just call Gabriel Kruger 'Gabe?'"

00:09:28.720 --> 00:09:33.680
"Yeah. We're on a first-name&nbsp;
basis. It's a bromance! You know!"

00:09:34.702 --> 00:09:39.760
Valve Corporation's legendary first-person shooter&nbsp;
Half-Life changed the video game industry forever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.760 --> 00:09:45.680
when it hit personal computers in 1998. It tells&nbsp;
the heart-pounding story of theoretical physicist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.680 --> 00:09:50.960
Gordon Freeman and his dimension-shattering&nbsp;
discoveries at the Black Mesa Research Facility.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.960 --> 00:09:55.680
And curiously enough, Black Mesa would prove to&nbsp;
be part of the game's connective tissue to another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.680 --> 00:10:01.120
Valve title, Portal, the award-winning&nbsp;
3D puzzle-platformer released in 2007.

00:10:01.120 --> 00:10:04.960
Portal takes players on a wild ride&nbsp;
through the Aperture Science laboratories&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.960 --> 00:10:09.200
as they try to unravel the enigmas of the&nbsp;
artificial intelligence known as GLaDOS&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.200 --> 00:10:13.040
and learn for themselves that the cake&nbsp;
is a lie. Astute gamers will recognize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.040 --> 00:10:16.880
oblique clues in GLaDOS' dialogue that&nbsp;
reference the events of Half-Life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.880 --> 00:10:20.320
along with a tongue-in-cheek&nbsp;
PowerPoint presentation. Furthermore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.320 --> 00:10:26.000
Aperture founder Cave Johnson mentions his Black&nbsp;
Mesa rivals by name multiple times in Portal 2.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:31.360
Whether or not industrial espionage is healthy for&nbsp;
business, it certainly made for one sweet spinoff.

00:10:31.360 --> 00:10:34.800
"Cave Johnson, new owner and CEO of&nbsp;
Black Mesa. That's right. You've been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:37.920
bought. First order of business: We're&nbsp;
renaming you under the Aperture brand."

00:10:38.160 --> 00:10:43.120
Due in 2022, The Callisto Protocol is a&nbsp;
survival horror game set in the furthest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.120 --> 00:10:47.600
reaches of outer space. The game places players&nbsp;
in the shoes of a prisoner in Black Iron,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.600 --> 00:10:52.560
a prison located on Jupiter's moon Callisto.&nbsp;
When aliens attack the facility, it's up to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.560 --> 00:10:56.960
the prisoner to fight the invasion and get&nbsp;
out alive. Developed by Striking Distance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.960 --> 00:11:01.040
and directed by Dead Space co-creator&nbsp;
Glen Schofield, the terrifying-looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.040 --> 00:11:05.520
game seems like a far cry from the setting of&nbsp;
PlayerUnknown's Battlegrounds. However, as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.520 --> 00:11:10.880
Striking Distance rep confirmed for IGN, the two&nbsp;
games are actually linked by a shared universe.

00:11:10.880 --> 00:11:15.440
In an interview with GamesRadar, Schofield said&nbsp;
that although a few hundred years and plenty of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.440 --> 00:11:20.800
space separates PUBG and The Callisto Protocol,&nbsp;
hardcore fans would most likely be able to pick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.800 --> 00:11:25.440
up on references to the wider PUBG universe&nbsp;
throughout the game. Schofield explained:

00:11:25.440 --> 00:11:30.240
"We're helping PUBG Corp right now as a team of&nbsp;
writers, working on the lore for PUBG and its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.240 --> 00:11:34.400
universe. It's not going to be really deep, but&nbsp;
there will be little connections here and there."

